[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `SIGPIPE`: Rust exited 134 with a panic message where C exited 141 silently
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom** — with the write end of stdout attached to an already-closed reader:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ ./c_src/build/driver < in | true            ; # C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit status 141 (killed by SIGPIPE), stderr empty
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ ./translation/target/release/driver < in | true   ; # Rust, before the fix
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit status 134 (SIGABRT), stderr:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m thread 'main' panicked at library/std/src/io/stdio.rs: failed printing to stdout: Broken pipe (os error 32)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause** — Rust's runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before entering `main`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so a failed write surfaces as `EPIPE`; `println!` panics on write errors, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `panic = "abort"` turns that into `SIGABRT`. A C program inherits the default
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m disposition and is killed by the signal instead, and C code that ignores
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf`'s return value never notices a write error at all.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix** — `cruntime::reset_sigpipe()` restores `SIG_DFL` for `SIGPIPE` as the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m first statement of `main`, and the flush path discards write errors the way the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C code ignores `printf`'s return value.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `broken_pipe_exit_status_matches` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `broken_pipe_produces_no_rust_panic_message`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. Output buffering discipline differed from C's stdio
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom** — a partially-consuming reader observed different behaviour, e.g.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `driver | head -1`: C finished with status 0, Rust was killed at status 141.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause** — Rust's `println!` writes through a `LineWriter`, which flushes on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m every newline regardless of what stdout is. glibc chooses at first use: line
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m buffered only when stdout is a terminal, otherwise fully buffered (`BUFSIZ`),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so this program's ~130 bytes of output reach a pipe in a single write at exit.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m With per-line writes, Rust hit the closed pipe on its second line while C never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m performed a failing write at all.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix** — `cruntime` emulates the C stream: output accumulates in a buffer that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is flushed when it reaches `BUFSIZ` or when `main` exits, and only flushes per
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m line when `isatty(1)`. Verified against the C binary both through a pipe and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m through a pty.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `broken_pipe_produces_no_rust_panic_message` (and the pty check
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m described above, which is not part of the test suite because it needs a tty).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours deliberately preserved, not "fixed"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are not bugs in the translation; they are the C program's behaviour and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the Rust code reproduces them on purpose.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Divide by zero in `bad()`.** `100.0 / 0.0F` is `+inf`, and `(int)` of an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   out-of-range double is undefined behaviour in C. The reference build on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   x86-64 emits `cvttsd2si`, which yields the "integer indefinite" value
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `INT_MIN`, so the program prints `-2147483648`. `cruntime::f64_to_int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   returns `i32::MIN` for NaN and for anything outside `[-2^31, 2^31)`, matching
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the C binary for every input tried, including the exact boundary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `100.0 / 0x1.9p-25 == 2147483648.0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`fgets`, not `scanf`.** Reading stops at a newline and never crosses one;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   at most `CHAR_ARRAY_SIZE - 1 == 19` bytes are stored per call, and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   remainder of an over-long line is picked up by the *next* call. So
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `1\n2\n3\n` gives `goodB2G()` the value `1` and `bad()` the value `2`, and a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   25-character line is split between the two.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Two independent reads.** `goodB2G()` reads first, then `bad()`. One line of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   input therefore prints `fgets() failed.` from `bad()` only.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`fabs(data) > 0.000001` is a strict comparison**, so exactly `0.000001`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   takes the "This would result in a divide by zero" branch. `NaN` also takes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   that branch, because every comparison with `NaN` is false.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`atof` == `strtod(s, NULL)`**: leading whitespace and sign, hex (`0x1.8p3`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and `inf`/`nan` forms are accepted, the longest valid prefix wins
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`12abc` → `12`), incomplete exponents are not consumed (`5e` → `5`), and an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   unconvertible string yields `0.0` rather than an error.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`(float)atof(...)` truncates to single precision** before the division, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `1e-40` becomes a subnormal float and `1e-300` becomes `0.0F` — which is why
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   those inputs reach the divide-by-zero paths.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **A NUL byte ends the C string** even though `fgets` stored bytes past it, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `1\x002` converts as `1`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`printLine(NULL)` is unreachable** in this program: every call site passes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   a literal. The `Option`-taking signature is kept for fidelity but only the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `Some` arm is exercised, so there is no observable behaviour to compare.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`argc`/`argv` are ignored**, so arguments cannot change the output.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Status
